@startuml "Bridge - Notifications (Problem) - Class Diagram"
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
title Before — three naive answers to two independent axes


package "1 - one class, a switch on each axis" {
  class SwitchingNotifier {
    + Send(kind, channel, to, message) : DeliveryResult
    - SendSimple(channel, ..) : DeliveryResult
    - SendUrgent(channel, ..) : DeliveryResult
    - SendDigest(channel, ..) : DeliveryResult
  }
  note bottom of SwitchingNotifier
    3 kinds x 3 channels = <b>9 branches</b>, by hand.
    No edit touches one axis alone.

    Three of those branches send an SMS, and the
    160-character rule is stated in <b>two</b> of them.
    SendDigest is the one that forgot, so an SMS
    digest throws — in production, at 2am.
    A test counts the branches and the rule.
  end note
}

package "2 - a class per (kind, channel) pair" {
  class UrgentEmailNotification
  class UrgentSmsNotification
  class DigestEmailNotification
  class "...six more..." as More
  note bottom of More
    The class name has to name <b>both axes</b>,
    so the class count is their product:
    3 x 3 = <b>9 classes</b>, and the retry loop
    is written once per channel.
  end note
}

package "3 - the notification inherits the channel" {
  abstract class EmailSender {
    # Deliver(address, subject, body) : bool
    # ChannelName : string
  }
  class EmailBoundUrgentNotification {
    + Send(to, message) : DeliveryResult
  }
  EmailSender <|-- EmailBoundUrgentNotification
  note bottom of EmailBoundUrgentNotification
    <b>The channel is the type, not a field.</b>
    EmailBoundUrgentNotification is an EmailSender,
    not "has a channel", so a preference read from a database cannot
    reach this object at any price: a base class
    is chosen when the code compiles, and never
    again.

    <b>The burden moves to every call site.</b>
    The symptom is one silent email; the disease is
    that avoiding the mismatch is now each caller's
    job, and no compiler checks the one that forgot.
  end note
}

' Graphviz places packages by content, not by declaration order, and put the
' second one first. Hidden edges pin them to the order the deck teaches them in.
SwitchingNotifier -[hidden]right- UrgentEmailNotification
DigestEmailNotification -[hidden]right- EmailSender

note as N1
  All three work. All three make the same mistake in different
  handwriting: <b>the two axes are welded together</b>, so neither
  can change without disturbing the other.
end note

@enduml
